crypto/tls: sign with keys of types this package doesn't implement

A Certificate's PrivateKey can be any crypto.Signer, but crypto/tls
derives the signature schemes, the cipher suites and the certificate
selection from the type of its public key, and rejects other types. A
hardware or smart card key whose public key type crypto/tls doesn't know,
such as an ECDSA key on a curve that crypto/ecdsa doesn't implement, can't
be used, although crypto/tls only needs the key to sign. Issue #28660
added SignMessage support for such signers; this is the follow-up for
their public key types.

For a PrivateKey whose public key is of a type this package doesn't
implement, Certificate.SupportedSignatureAlgorithms becomes required and
authoritative: it lists the schemes the key can sign with. In TLS 1.2,
the listed schemes that this package implements are used; they must be
all ECDSA or all RSA (PKCS #1 v1.5 or RSA-PSS) schemes, and that
signature type selects the cipher suites, as the public key type does
for other keys.
ClientHelloInfo.SupportsCertificate and
CertificateRequestInfo.SupportsCertificate check the schemes, but not the
key's curve against the client's supported groups, since the curve is
unknown; a GetCertificate callback can add that check. Signing in TLS 1.2
goes through crypto.SignMessage with the scheme's hash, as for other keys.

In TLS 1.3, the schemes this package implements are bound to key types it
implements (an ECDSA scheme to its curve), so such a key signs only with
the listed schemes that this package doesn't implement, such as
ecdsa_brainpoolP256r1tls13_sha256 (RFC 8734). This package doesn't know
their hash, so the key must be a crypto.MessageSigner: SignMessage gets
the unhashed content and a new *SignatureSchemeOptions that names the
scheme; its HashFunc returns zero. A dedicated type, rather than making
SignatureScheme itself a crypto.SignerOpts, keeps every SignatureScheme
from being accepted as signer options: rsa.PrivateKey.Sign(rand, digest,
PSSWithSHA256) would compile and silently make a PKCS #1 v1.5 signature.
A peer advertises such schemes only if it can verify them.

Such certificates are not used before TLS 1.2 or in FIPS 140-3 mode.

The tests wrap a P-256 key in a signer whose Public method returns an
unknown type, so peers verify its signatures as usual. In TLS 1.3, the
signer uses a private-use scheme for P-256 with SHA-256, its certificate
names a made-up curve, and the peer verifies it with
Config.VerifyUnsupportedSignature.

ivory: P3, profiles core and kt. Base: go1.27.2 with P0-P2. Upstream:
proposal B. Requirements: A_17089-03, A_22453 (gemSpec_Krypt V2.51.0,
gemSpec_KT V3.17.1).
Status: TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3.

